import"./NZTpNUN0.js";import"./BkvxQF8z.js";import{t,P as n,q as o}from"./D3cAVrQp.js";var r=t("<h1>Integrated Meta-Governance Framework v1.6.1</h1> <h2>A delegated coordination architecture for plural institutions, bounded learning, and lawful adaptation</h2> <p><strong>Tier:</strong> 1 — Constitutional Foundation <strong>Status:</strong> Representation-authority boundary clarification patch <strong>Revision:</strong> 1.6.1 (2026-08-08) <strong>Responsible:</strong> Global Governance Frameworks initiative, developed with AI assistance</p> <h3>Related frameworks</h3> <ul><li><strong>Treaty for Our Only Home</strong> — constitutional authority, institutions, jurisdiction, emergency powers, finance, and entry into force</li> <li><strong>Constitutional Interface Specification</strong> — controlling constitutional rules for legitimacy, jurisdiction, causal responsibility, response duties, proportionality, observation, transfer, and dissolution</li> <li><strong>Governance Method, Experiment, Adoption, and Implementation Authority Interface Specification (GMEAIA)</strong> — controlling lifecycle grammar for authority effects, handoffs, response states, consequence profiles, causal-responsibility records, and closure</li> <li><strong>Indigenous Sovereignty and Planetary Healing</strong> — Indigenous sovereignty, consent, protected knowledge, and co-development limits</li> <li><strong>Polycentric Governance Architecture</strong> — horizontal territorial, commons, practice, and Indigenous interfaces, subject to the Constitutional Interface Specification</li> <li><strong>AUBI Framework</strong> — material security, population-level observation, epistemic rights, and protected unobservability</li> <li><strong>Financial Systems Framework</strong> — Hearts and Leaves operations, financial observability, crisis liquidity, and change governance</li> <li><strong>Nested Economies Framework</strong> — economic subsidiarity, cross-scale interfaces, and voluntary coordination layers</li> <li><strong>Work in Liberation Framework</strong> — worker rights, Community Providers, need broadcasting, and voluntary contribution</li> <li><strong>Universal Access and Disability Justice Layer</strong> — accessibility, cognitive liberty, and universal design</li></ul> <h3>Version history</h3> <ul><li><strong>1.6.1 (2026-08-08):</strong> Representation-authority boundary clarification following the corpus-wide electoral representation, constituency, and institutional-selection audit. Adds §3.7 to make explicit what §3.1, §3.5, and the Indigenous-authority rule already implied: frameworks may identify representation needs and propose composition profiles, but a seat ratio, quota, selector, voting weight, nomination rule, term, recall rule, or vacancy rule becomes binding only through the valid constituting instrument of the body concerned. Prohibits one framework from prescribing the composition of a body constituted by another framework, the Treaty, an Indigenous nation, municipality, state, union, cooperative, or other independent authority. Distinguishes constituency representation, affectedness, expertise, delegated institutional authority, protected-interest guardianship, sortition, and advisory participation. Reaffirms that Indigenous participation follows affected peoples’ own authority rather than GGF quotas or proxy appointment; that selection determines who may occupy an office but does not expand the office’s jurisdiction; and that representation is not consent, FPIC, Treaty accession, territorial delegation, or waiver of individual rights. Extends the existing Meta-Governance Charter Record and conformance requirements rather than creating a new representation record family. Adds a bounded migration rule for inherited composition formulas. No electoral-administration, voter-roll, ballot, campaign, certification, recount, or universal franchise machinery is created.</li> <li><strong>1.6 (2026-08-05):</strong> Recursive systems-audit integration following the four-framework review of Meta-Governance, Nested Economies, Institutional Regeneration, and Implementation & Adaptation, and adoption of Constitutional Interface Specification v0.2.1 and GMEAIA v0.2.1. Added causal responsibility and jointly constituted authority; duties attached to retained jurisdiction; response, refusal, capacity-limit, timeout, and constitutional-gap handling; consequence-scaled documentation; coordination-added latency and response clocks; common-mode dependency and objective-reproduction review; minimum necessary documentation; material closure support; framework-level falsification; and evaluation of coordination avoided, simplified, devolved, or ended. Preserved the connector rule and prohibition on automatic authority transfer. This Markdown successor is semantically migration-requiring; downstream schemas and manifests remain pinned to their prior compatible versions until separately regenerated.</li> <li><strong>Editorial edition (2026-08-02).</strong> No substantive change. Removed version pins from the related-frameworks list and body prose so that a reference names an instrument rather than asserting a live dependency that goes stale when a sibling framework moves; editions consulted are recorded in this version history instead. Corrected the tier label from 4 (a remnant of an earlier architecture) to 1 — Constitutional Foundation, matching this framework’s actual position. Corrected a stale reference to an earlier Polycentric edition. Editions consulted: Treaty for Our Only Home v1.3; Constitutional Interface Specification v0.1; Indigenous Sovereignty and Planetary Healing v1.1; Polycentric Governance Architecture v0.6; AUBI v2.5; Financial Systems v3.4; Nested Economies v3.2; Work in Liberation v2.0.</li> <li><strong>1.5 (2026-08-01):</strong> Constitutional alignment revision following the Constitutional Stack Coherence, Sovereignty, and Observability Audit v0.1. Recast Meta-Governance as a delegated connector rather than an independent source of substantive authority. Replaced the scalar Learning Velocity Index and automatic Adaptation Deficit Trigger with a multidimensional Learning and Adaptation Profile and authorized review process. Rebuilt Variety Gap Assessment as plural, decision-specific, and non-self-executing. Removed automatic constitutional-unobservability declarations, forced reintegration, authority-transfer pressure ramps, automatic funding reallocation, and unilateral institutional dissolution. Added decision-chain separation, Observation Contracts, State Estimate Records, Observation Independence Maps, polycentric interface records, protected non-transfer, lawful bypass options, stable coexistence, succession and dissolution continuity, rights-bounded emergency coordination, Indigenous recognition rather than representation quotas, and explicit conformance to Treaty v1.3.</li> <li><strong>1.4 (2026-05-19):</strong> Systemic dynamics upgrade introducing Adaptive Learning Velocity, Bypass Architecture, Coexistence and Transition, Independent Dissolution Authority, Variety Gap Assessment, and Fractal Governance Bandwidth.</li> <li><strong>1.3 (2026-03-26):</strong> Added cognitive equity provisions, Multi-Modal Deliberation, System Sanity and Cognitive Friction Audits, and System Canary roles.</li> <li><strong>1.2 (2026-03-24):</strong> Reframed coordination through Accountable Coordination and removed several unmeasurable metrics.</li> <li><strong>1.1 (2025-11-05):</strong> Added Governance Immune System concepts and conscious-refuser protections.</li> <li><strong>1.0 (2025-10-31):</strong> First public release.</li></ul> <p><strong>Source freeze:</strong> Integrated Meta-Governance v1.6 SHA-256 <code>cf1cd81a681b6b94ef4107cee35dfdabb3a991f6472c75d87fa7218a229466ba</code><br> <strong>Controlling interface freeze for this bounded patch:</strong> Constitutional Interface Specification v0.2.1 SHA-256 <code>2ab06be70d568be82efde2ccbcc3f8df48f37308fab3ccf116ac3efe4c167bfc</code>; GMEAIA v0.2.1 SHA-256 <code>8cdaf8c316b50b251721a824124e47b1ad30787e9f5281d7fade0f4360bfe5d1</code></p> <hr> <h1>Layer 1 — Meta-Governance Lite</h1> <h2>What is Meta-Governance?</h2> <p>Meta-Governance is the <strong>coordination of governance systems</strong>.</p> <p>Health, climate, finance, migration, technology, peace, work, Indigenous sovereignty, and ecological stewardship each have their own institutions, knowledge, rights, and responsibilities. Their decisions nevertheless affect one another. Meta-Governance provides the interfaces through which those systems can:</p> <ul><li>recognize interdependence;</li> <li>exchange bounded information;</li> <li>identify jurisdictional overlap;</li> <li>convene the authorities that already possess legitimate standing;</li> <li>compare interpretations without forcing premature consensus;</li> <li>coordinate shared action where authority has been delegated;</li> <li>learn from failure;</li> <li>support experiments;</li> <li>return or dissolve temporary coordination structures when their work ends.</li></ul> <p>Meta-Governance is <strong>not a world government above other governments</strong>. It does not gain power merely because a problem crosses domains or because its observers believe another institution is failing.</p> <h2>The jazz ensemble analogy—revised</h2> <p>A jazz ensemble coordinates without every musician becoming the conductor.</p> <ul><li>Each musician retains a distinct instrument, skill, and responsibility.</li> <li>Shared tempo and listening make joint action possible.</li> <li>A facilitator may help the ensemble hear conflicts or transitions.</li> <li>The facilitator does not own the music or acquire the instruments.</li> <li>A musician may refuse a cue that violates the agreed score or the conditions of participation.</li> <li>When the piece ends, the temporary coordination arrangement ends with it.</li></ul> <p>The analogy has limits. Governance includes rights, coercive power, unequal resources, contested jurisdiction, and people who did not choose the performance. Meta-Governance must therefore operate through constitutional authority, not harmony language alone.</p> <h2>Eight quick rules</h2> <ol><li><strong>Coordinate only what is genuinely interdependent.</strong></li> <li><strong>Use the minimum authority, information, and documentation necessary.</strong></li> <li><strong>Keep observation, interpretation, authorization, implementation, and adjudication distinct.</strong></li> <li><strong>Treat indicators as alerts, not judgments.</strong></li> <li><strong>Assign responsibility according to causal control, lawful jurisdiction, practical capacity, and resources.</strong></li> <li><strong>Make acceptance, refusal, delay, incapacity, and constitutional absence visible without transferring authority by silence.</strong></li> <li><strong>Treat representation needs as design requirements, not self-executing authority to compose another institution.</strong></li> <li><strong>Return authority and dissolve temporary structures when the reason for them ends.</strong></li></ol> <h2>What Meta-Governance may do</h2> <p>Meta-Governance may:</p> <ul><li>map interfaces and dependencies;</li> <li>convene affected authorities;</li> <li>facilitate negotiation and mediation;</li> <li>maintain constitutional and operational interface records;</li> <li>support plural modelling and foresight;</li> <li>identify possible blind spots;</li> <li>host bounded pilots where lawfully authorized;</li> <li>coordinate voluntary shared services;</li> <li>publish non-binding audits and dissent;</li> <li>monitor whether coordination arrangements remain accountable, timely, and causally adequate;</li> <li>record response, refusal, delay, capacity limitation, and constitutional gaps through the controlling interface;</li> <li>prepare options for authorized decision-makers;</li> <li>identify when coordination should be avoided, simplified, devolved, federated, or ended.</li></ul> <h2>What Meta-Governance may not do by itself</h2> <p>Meta-Governance may not, solely through its own framework or diagnosis:</p> <ul><li>legislate;</li> <li>tax;</li> <li>impose sanctions;</li> <li>command police, military, emergency, or work forces;</li> <li>determine criminal or civil liability;</li> <li>suspend rights;</li> <li>invalidate a currency or economic charter;</li> <li>override Indigenous sovereignty or consent;</li> <li>acquire another institution’s jurisdiction;</li> <li>dissolve another institution;</li> <li>force a pilot to reintegrate;</li> <li>turn a dashboard threshold into a constitutional judgment;</li> <li>treat another institution’s silence, refusal, incapacity, or missed deadline as consent or as an automatic transfer of jurisdiction;</li> <li>certify its own coordination model as legitimate merely because it conforms to GGF records.</li></ul> <p>Those actions require authority from the Treaty, a valid compact, an Indigenous authority, a democratic territorial body, a tribunal, or another institution that legitimately holds the relevant power.</p> <h2>How to participate</h2> <ul><li><strong>Explore:</strong> inspect maps, records, disagreements, and public reasons.</li> <li><strong>Contribute:</strong> provide evidence, lived experience, technical analysis, or facilitation.</li> <li><strong>Co-design:</strong> participate in a time-limited, authorized interface or pilot.</li> <li><strong>Challenge:</strong> contest assumptions, methods, standing, or authority.</li> <li><strong>Refuse:</strong> decline participation where the process violates rights, consent, or Accountable Coordination.</li></ul> <p>Participation status is not a hierarchy of human worth. Refusal, withdrawal, and non-participation do not imply ignorance, obstruction, or lack of solidarity.</p> <hr> <h1>Layer 2 — Constitutional and Operational Architecture</h1> <h1>1. Purpose, status, and constitutional position</h1> <h2>1.1 Purpose</h2> <p>This framework governs the design and operation of <strong>coordination interfaces among institutions that retain their own jurisdiction</strong>.</p> <p>Its purposes are to:</p> <ul><li>reduce destructive conflict among legitimate authorities;</li> <li>enable joint action on cross-domain and cross-scale conditions;</li> <li>preserve local, professional, ecological, Indigenous, and democratic knowledge;</li> <li>expose blind spots without claiming total visibility;</li> <li>improve institutional learning without rewarding surveillance;</li> <li>enable experimentation without manufacturing permanent shadow sovereignty;</li> <li>support lawful succession and graceful dissolution;</li> <li>prevent coordination bodies from converting interdependence into self-expanding authority.</li></ul> <h2>1.2 Constitutional subordination</h2> <p>This framework does not create independent constitutional authority.</p> <p>Meta-Governance institutions operate only through:</p> <ol><li>authority expressly conferred by the Treaty for Our Only Home;</li> <li>a lawful compact among participating authorities;</li> <li>an Indigenous authority’s specific and continuing consent;</li> <li>a domain framework within its valid competence;</li> <li>a tribunal order or lawful public mandate;</li> <li>voluntary participation where no coercive power is exercised.</li></ol> <p>Where this framework conflicts with the Treaty or the Constitutional Interface Specification, those documents govern. GMEAIA governs the operational semantics of authority effects, consequence profiles, handoffs, response states, clocks, record custody, and closure used by this framework.</p> <h2>1.3 Connector rule</h2> <p>Meta-Governance is a <strong>connector, not a residual sovereign</strong>.</p> <p>A matter does not become a Meta-Governance competence merely because:</p> <ul><li>several domains are involved;</li> <li>the problem is complex;</li> <li>the existing institutions disagree;</li> <li>a coordination council has better data;</li> <li>an audit concludes that another institution is slow, captured, or unobservable;</li> <li>the coordination body can imagine a more coherent solution.</li></ul> <p>The institution holding lawful competence retains jurisdiction unless authority is validly delegated, transferred, or jointly constituted through the procedures governing that competence.</p> <p>Retained jurisdiction does not imply unlimited practical capacity. A consequential referral shall identify whether a response duty exists, its source and effect, and the applicable acknowledgment and decision clocks. Silence, refusal, timeout, or incapacity does not transfer authority by implication.</p> <h2>1.4 Normative language</h2> <ul><li><strong>Must</strong> and <strong>shall</strong> identify requirements for conformance with this framework.</li> <li><strong>May</strong> identifies a permitted option.</li> <li><strong>Should</strong> identifies a strong design recommendation that may require contextual adaptation.</li> <li><strong>Proposal</strong> identifies a mechanism without presently constituted authority.</li></ul> <h2>1.5 Scope boundaries</h2> <p>This framework does not define:</p> <ul><li>the substantive content of human rights or criminal law;</li> <li>Indigenous governance institutions or representatives;</li> <li>Hearts or Leaves monetary operations;</li> <li>AUBI eligibility and benefit rules;</li> <li>professional and labour standards;</li> <li>ecological thresholds;</li> <li>military or police doctrine;</li> <li>voter registration, electoral rolls, ballot administration, districting, campaign law, vote counting, certification, recounts, or other ordinary electoral administration;</li> <li>the validity of scientific, spiritual, cultural, or Traditional Knowledge claims.</li></ul> <p>It governs how authorized institutions coordinate around those subjects.</p> <h1>2. Core principles</h1> <h2>2.1 Accountable Coordination</h2> <p>Every coordination mechanism shall be tested against four questions:</p> <ol><li><strong>Is its authority revocable and externally grounded, or self-justifying?</strong></li> <li><strong>Is transparency meaningfully symmetric, or does the institution observe others while remaining opaque?</strong></li> <li><strong>Are exit and challenge practically available, or prohibitively costly?</strong></li> <li><strong>Is the mechanism self-limiting, or does institutional survival become its primary function?</strong></li></ol> <p>Failure of the test is an alert requiring review. It does not automatically transfer authority or dissolve the mechanism.</p> <h2>2.2 Polycentric coordination</h2> <p>Coordination should connect multiple centers of authority without collapsing them into one chain of command.</p> <p>Relevant centers may include:</p> <ul><li>territorial democratic authorities;</li> <li>cross-boundary Commons Trusts;</li> <li>Indigenous nations and authorities;</li> <li>labour, guild, professional, and provider institutions;</li> <li>Treaty institutions;</li> <li>tribunals;</li> <li>scientific and knowledge bodies;</li> <li>youth and future-generations institutions;</li> <li>civil society and affected-community bodies.</li></ul> <h2>2.3 Causal subsidiarity and capability</h2> <p>Decisions remain at the lowest effective scale consistent with rights, shared-commons obligations, and material cross-boundary effects. Responsibility allocation shall also examine:</p> <ul><li>control over principal causal variables;</li> <li>lawful jurisdiction;</li> <li>practical capacity;</li> <li>resources available to discharge the duty;</li> <li>upstream and cross-boundary effects.</li></ul> <p>The place where consequences appear does not establish where responsibility belongs. A body is not capable merely because it can administer or absorb consequences generated elsewhere.</p> <p>A lower-capacity institution should receive assistance before losing authority. Staffing shortages, technical limitations, or unequal resources are not by themselves proof that centralization is necessary.</p> <p>Where a condition exists only through relations among authorities—such as a watershed, grid, settlement network, labour market, transport corridor, or shared infrastructure—authority may be jointly constituted rather than described as a local power transferred upward.</p> <h2>2.4 Bounded coordination</h2> <p>No coordination body, dashboard, secretariat, council, or AI system shall be designed to obtain full visibility or control over the entire governance system.</p> <p>Every consequential representation shall disclose:</p> <ul><li>uncertainty;</li> <li>known blind spots;</li> <li>missing populations;</li> <li>source dependencies;</li> <li>competing interpretations;</li> <li>conditions under which the representation would be revised.</li></ul> <h2>2.5 Dynamic interoperability</h2> <p>Institutions should be able to cooperate without adopting one ontology, technology, administrative culture, or value metric.</p> <p>Interoperability may include recognition, authentication, bounded attestation, settlement, consultation, translation, or coordinated action. Permission for one does not imply permission for all.</p> <h2>2.6 Sensemaking sovereignty</h2> <p>Affected people and institutions retain standing to describe conditions in their own terms, submit counter-models, identify translation loss, and withhold knowledge where no overriding lawful requirement exists.</p> <p>A global or technical model does not become authoritative merely because it is standardized, quantified, or computationally sophisticated.</p> <h2>2.7 Epistemic plurality</h2> <p>Consequential coordination should use multiple genuinely independent observation and modelling channels. Their disagreement shall be surfaced rather than averaged away when the disagreement is decision-relevant.</p> <p>Plurality is not satisfied by several models trained on the same data, managed by the same institution, or rewarded by the same objective.</p> <h2>2.8 Cognitive equity and universal access</h2> <p>Coordination processes shall support synchronous, asynchronous, visual, oral, narrative, embodied, supported, and low-technology participation where relevant.</p> <p>Accessibility is not an optional accommodation added after institutional design. It is part of whether authority is legitimate and whether the system can observe its own effects.</p> <h2>2.9 Human authority and bounded automation</h2> <p>AI and automated systems may support analysis, translation, anomaly detection, simulation, and administrative work. They may not independently make ethical, constitutional, jurisdictional, identity, consent, sanction, or dissolution judgments.</p> <h2>2.10 Planetary stewardship and rights</h2> <p>Coordination shall respect the Treaty’s rights, commons, ecological, and intergenerational obligations. Meta-Governance does not define those obligations but must not design interfaces that make them unenforceable.</p> <h2>2.11 Economic sufficiency</h2> <p>Coordination infrastructure should serve human and ecological flourishing rather than institutional expansion, transaction volume, or indefinite economic growth.</p> <h2>2.12 Peace and regeneration</h2> <p>Mediation, prevention, restorative processes, and nonviolent resolution are preferred where consistent with rights and safety. Coordination must not use harmony language to suppress legitimate conflict, resistance, or accountability.</p> <h2>2.13 Learning without surveillance</h2> <p>Institutional learning shall be judged by improved detection, correction, adaptation, and outcomes—not by sensor count, data volume, or the breadth of centralized visibility.</p> <h2>2.14 Lawful experimentation</h2> <p>Experiments shall be bounded by purpose, authority, rights, duration, evidence, challenge, and exit. Successful experiments earn consideration, not automatic jurisdiction.</p> <h2>2.15 Duty attached to retained jurisdiction</h2> <p>A Meta-Governance process shall not manufacture a response duty. It shall disclose whether a duty exists through law, treaty, charter, compact, contract, internal rule, or voluntary commitment.</p> <p>Where such a duty exists, the responsible institution should acknowledge a properly routed consequential request and accept, refuse, defer, refer, or identify a capacity limitation within a decision-appropriate period. Reasons, conflicts, expected harm during delay, provisional protections, and escalation routes shall remain visible.</p> <h2>2.16 Proportional procedural burden</h2> <p>Record depth shall be proportional to reversibility, rights and service effects, coercion, public-law effect, exposure of non-participants, ecological irreversibility, data sensitivity, duration, and scale.</p> <p>Existing valid records shall be reused. Low-risk voluntary coordination shall not require dissolution-grade process. High-consequence coordination shall not evade safeguards because its method is technically simple.</p> <p>The institution proposing coordination bears the primary burden of showing necessity, preparing accessible documentation, and supporting weaker participants’ independent review.</p> <h2>2.17 Conformance is not success</h2> <p>Conformance shows that authority, process, rights, and lifecycle requirements were represented. It does not establish legitimacy, causal effectiveness, justice, trust, ecological improvement, or the success of Meta-Governance itself.</p> <h2>2.18 Liberatory impermanence</h2> <p>Coordination institutions are provisional concentrations of capacity. Their continuation must remain justified. Dissolution, federation, devolution, and protocol transfer are legitimate outcomes when the originating need changes or ends.</p> <h1>3. Coordination actors</h1> <h2>3.1 Meta-Governance Coordination Councils</h2> <p>A Meta-Governance Coordination Council is a convening and interface body created by the Treaty, a compact, or participating authorities.</p> <p>Its charter must identify:</p> <ul><li>the condition or interface it coordinates;</li> <li>participating institutions;</li> <li>affected non-participants;</li> <li>authority formation: retained, delegated, transferred, concurrent, or jointly constituted;</li> <li>delegated powers and prohibited powers;</li> <li>representation functions where membership is consequential: constituency, affectedness, expertise, delegated institutional authority, protected-interest guardianship, sortition, or advisory participation;</li> <li>whether any composition profile is enacted by the constituting instrument or remains an illustrative design proposal;</li> <li>the authorized selector or selection process for each consequential seat or role;</li> <li>term, vacancy, replacement, recall, removal, and mandate-expiry rules where applicable;</li> <li>causal variables and responsible controllers where material;</li> <li>applicable response duties and clocks;</li> <li>consequence profile under GMEAIA;</li> <li>duration;</li> <li>funding;</li> <li>decision rules;</li> <li>challenge and appeal;</li> <li>review, devolution, and closure conditions.</li></ul> <p>A council does not possess a general power to resolve every issue arising within its field. Nor may it treat its own preferred composition as evidence that the relevant constituency, selector, or appointment authority has been lawfully constituted.</p> <h2>3.2 Interface Secretariats</h2> <p>An Interface Secretariat may maintain records, schedule deliberation, support translation, publish decisions, track commitments, and administer shared services.</p> <p>It may not determine jurisdictional disputes, treat record completion as substantive success, or expand its mandate through administrative interpretation. Translation, documentation, and routine interface costs should normally be borne by the coordination arrangement rather than shifted onto lower-capacity participants.</p> <h2>3.3 Domain Interface Panels</h2> <p>A Domain Interface Panel brings together authorities from distinct domains when one decision materially affects another.</p> <p>Examples include:</p> <ul><li>health and migration;</li> <li>finance and AUBI;</li> <li>technology and Indigenous knowledge;</li> <li>work and crisis response;</li> <li>climate and food systems;</li> <li>territorial planning and cross-boundary commons.</li></ul> <p>Panels prepare options and interface records. They do not replace the institutions that authorize action.</p> <h2>3.4 Global foresight function</h2> <p>The Global Council for the Future under Treaty supplies the principal Treaty-level foresight function. Meta-Governance may support a distributed network of foresight labs, knowledge institutions, Indigenous-authorized observers, civil-society analysts, and domain councils.</p> <p>The network shall not be collapsed into a single intelligence authority with privileged access to every domain.</p> <h2>3.5 Citizen and affected-community panels</h2> <p>Sortition panels, citizens’ assemblies, and affected-community bodies may:</p> <ul><li>identify lived impacts;</li> <li>review contested assumptions;</li> <li>participate in mediation;</li> <li>recommend action;</li> <li>exercise binding power where a valid constitution or compact expressly grants it.</li></ul> <p>Random selection does not make a body un-capturable, omnicompetent, or entitled to dissolve other institutions.</p> <h2>3.6 Office of the Adversary interface</h2> <p>The Office of the Adversary may inspect coordination claims, challenge assumptions, commission red teams, publish dissent, and identify suppressed alternatives.</p> <p>It does not adjudicate its own objections or direct remedial action.</p> <h2>3.7 Representation, composition, and institutional selection</h2> <p>A framework may identify <strong>representation requirements</strong> relevant to its function. It does not thereby acquire authority to constitute the represented constituency, select its representatives, or impose composition rules on another institution.</p> <p>The following rules apply:</p> <ol><li><strong>Own-body rule.</strong> A framework may propose, and where its valid constituting instrument permits may specify, the composition of a body genuinely constituted within that framework. A percentage, quota, voting weight, nomination rule, selection method, term, recall rule, or vacancy rule written in framework prose is not self-executing merely because it appears precise.</li> <li><strong>Other-body prohibition.</strong> No framework may prescribe seats, quotas, voting weights, appointment procedures, nomination routes, term rules, or membership requirements for a body constituted by another framework, the Treaty, an Indigenous nation, municipality, state, union, cooperative, professional body, or other independent authority unless that body’s own valid constituting instrument expressly adopts the rule.</li> <li><strong>Composition status.</strong> Unless independently enacted, numeric composition formulas are <strong>illustrative design profiles</strong>. They may express a domain’s legitimate need for affected-party voice, expertise, generational perspective, accessibility, territorial knowledge, or protected interests, but they do not create constitutional entitlements to seats.</li> <li><strong>Function declaration.</strong> Each consequential seat or role should state whether it represents a constituency, affectedness, delegated institutional authority, expertise, a protected interest, sortition-based public deliberation, or advisory knowledge. Expertise is not democratic representation; participation is not delegated authority.</li> <li><strong>Indigenous authority.</strong> Indigenous participation follows §3.8 and the relevant peoples’ own laws and processes. A GGF framework shall not manufacture a universal Indigenous electorate, impose an Indigenous seat percentage, certify an Indigenous proxy, or route appointment through an unrelated GGF body merely for administrative convenience.</li> <li><strong>Selection does not create jurisdiction.</strong> Election, sortition, nomination, appointment, confirmation, credentialing, seniority, or community designation determines who may occupy an office only to the extent the constituting instrument provides. It does not enlarge the office’s powers.</li> <li><strong>Representation is not consent.</strong> Representation does not by itself constitute FPIC, Treaty accession, territorial delegation, waiver of individual rights, consent of every constituent, or authority over non-constituents.</li> <li><strong>Lifecycle and dispute.</strong> Terms, resignation, recall or removal, vacancy, replacement, mandate expiry, contested selection, and failure to seat shall be assigned to competent authorities in the charter. Cross-framework handoff, response, refusal, capacity limitation, omission, unresolved-duty, and closure semantics remain governed by GMEAIA.</li></ol> <p>A domain framework may therefore say <strong>who or what must be meaningfully represented for its function to be legitimate</strong>. The constituting process determines <strong>how that requirement becomes a lawful composition and selection architecture</strong>.</p> <h2>3.8 Indigenous authorities</h2> <p>Meta-Governance recognizes Indigenous authorities identified through Indigenous peoples’ own processes. It shall not create a universal Indigenous council, impose a representation quota, certify traditional authority, or appoint proxies where no authorized Indigenous body has consented.</p> <p>Participation by an Indigenous authority in one council does not authorize representation of another people, territory, or institution. Reserved participation, where lawfully constituted, remains distinct from consent, FPIC, jurisdiction, and nation-to-nation authority.</p> <h2>3.9 System Canaries</h2> <p>A System Canary is a person or group whose experience may reveal contradictions, cognitive overload, moral injury, exclusion, or institutional unreality before conventional indicators respond.</p> <p>Canary testimony is evidence deserving protection and investigation. It is not automatically conclusive, and it must not be medicalized, romanticized, or used to place an unpaid diagnostic burden on vulnerable people.</p> <h1>4. Functional polycentrism and interface topology</h1> <h2>4.1 Horizontal dimensions</h2> <p>A coordination arrangement should identify at least four possible authority dimensions:</p> <ul><li><strong>territorial authority</strong> — place, infrastructure, services, and democratic jurisdiction;</li> <li><strong>commons authority</strong> — ecological systems and resources crossing territorial boundaries;</li> <li><strong>practice authority</strong> — work, professions, standards, and provider governance;</li> <li><strong>Indigenous sovereignty</strong> — inherent authority over peoples, territories, knowledge, and relations as determined by Indigenous institutions.</li></ul> <p>These dimensions may overlap without forming a fixed universal chamber system.</p> <h2>4.2 Vertical dimensions</h2> <p>Coordination may also occur among neighborhood, municipal or BAZ, bioregional, state, regional, Treaty, and intergenerational institutions.</p> <p>Scale does not itself determine superiority. A global institution may have wider jurisdiction over a shared condition but poorer local knowledge. A local institution may possess detailed knowledge but be unable to contain an externality.</p> <h2>4.3 No unilateral cross-domain action</h2> <p>An institution shall not use competence in one domain to determine another domain’s decision without valid authorization.</p> <p>For example:</p> <ul><li>a Treasury may process settlement but not decide the social worth of work;</li> <li>a labour body may set professional standards but not control a watershed;</li> <li>a Commons Trust may enjoin ecological harm but not redesign a community’s entire economy;</li> <li>a Meta-Governance council may identify interface failure but not acquire the affected institutions’ powers.</li></ul> <h2>4.4 Polycentric Interface Record</h2> <p>A consequential cross-domain arrangement shall publish a Polycentric Interface Record stating:</p> <ol><li>the condition requiring coordination;</li> <li>participating authorities and their standing;</li> <li>affected non-participants;</li> <li>decision class under Treaty;</li> <li>authority retained by each institution;</li> <li>authority delegated;</li> <li>information exchanged and withheld;</li> <li>disagreement and adjudication procedure;</li> <li>protected rights and minimums;</li> <li>exit, amendment, restoration, and closure conditions.</li></ol> <h2>4.5 Fractal adaptation</h2> <p>Small communities need not reproduce the full institutional form of a global system. Functions may be performed through distinct roles within one assembly if conflicts of interest remain visible and consequential decisions receive independent review.</p> <p>Fractal design preserves constitutional functions, not bureaucracy for its own sake.</p> <h2>4.6 Jointly constituted authority and constitutional gaps</h2> <p>Where the governance object exists only through relations among several jurisdictions, a Polycentric Interface Record shall state whether authority is concurrent or jointly constituted and identify the constituting source, parties, shared object, affected non-parties, exit conditions, and continuing duties.</p> <p>Where no identified authority possesses sufficient lawful jurisdiction, Meta-Governance may publish and route a Constitutional Gap Notice through GMEAIA-08 or GMEAIA-59, as applicable. The notice does not authorize the coordination body to fill the gap. It routes the matter toward lawful compact, legislation, treaty amendment, adjudication, or another constituting process.</p> <h1>5. The coordination lifecycle</h1> <h2>5.1 Trigger and consequence profile</h2> <p>Coordination may be initiated by:</p> <ul><li>a participating authority;</li> <li>an affected community;</li> <li>a Commons Trust;</li> <li>an Indigenous authority;</li> <li>a Treaty body;</li> <li>a tribunal;</li> <li>a worker or professional institution;</li> <li>an authorized sentinel or audit;</li> <li>a valid public petition.</li></ul> <p>A trigger opens inquiry. It does not establish the diagnosis or the coordinating body’s authority.</p> <p>The convenor shall select and justify the applicable GMEAIA consequence profile. Low-risk exploratory coordination may use a compact core record. Consequential or protected-domain coordination requires the additional authority, rights, monitoring, and closure functions triggered by its effects.</p> <h2>5.2 Standing, jurisdiction, and causal responsibility</h2> <p>Before substantive coordination begins, the convenor shall identify:</p> <ul><li>who claims standing;</li> <li>who is materially affected;</li> <li>which institutions hold legal and practical control over the principal causal variables;</li> <li>which institutions hold competence;</li> <li>whether any Indigenous nation or protected knowledge is implicated;</li> <li>whether the matter can be handled through support or voluntary coordination;</li> <li>whether responsibility is being assigned to an actor that can only absorb consequences;</li> <li>whether authority is retained, delegated, transferred, concurrent, jointly constituted, or absent;</li> <li>who may challenge the classification.</li></ul> <p>For consequential coordination, the process shall complete or reference the GMEAIA-20 Causal Responsibility Profile. An affected party’s standing creates participation, evidence, consent where applicable, challenge, and remedy rights; it does not itself assign responsibility.</p> <h2>5.3 Shared problem formulation</h2> <p>Participants shall document:</p> <ul><li>the condition to be addressed;</li> <li>competing descriptions;</li> <li>evidence and uncertainty;</li> <li>causal variables and uncontrolled dependencies;</li> <li>boundaries of the inquiry;</li> <li>excluded or unseen populations;</li> <li>the condition’s relevant timescale;</li> <li>what would count as improvement or failure;</li> <li>what the proposed coordination cannot establish.</li></ul> <p>The formulation may remain plural. Coordination does not require one agreed theory of the whole problem.</p> <h2>5.4 Response-time and handoff design</h2> <p>Before a consequential referral or handoff, the process shall identify:</p> <ul><li>the candidate recipient and basis for competence;</li> <li>whether a response duty exists and its source and effect;</li> <li>the legal, charter, compact, or GMEAIA-07 basis for acknowledgment and decision clocks;</li> <li>expected harm during delay;</li> <li>any pre-authorized safe state;</li> <li>sender and recipient duties;</li> <li>escalation and custody if the recipient refuses, defers, times out, or identifies a capacity limitation.</li></ul> <p>Exact response states and lifecycle semantics are governed by GMEAIA. Silence does not create acceptance, correctness, or authority transfer.</p> <h2>5.5 Option development</h2> <p>Options should distinguish:</p> <ul><li>local adaptation;</li> <li>capacity support;</li> <li>mutual aid;</li> <li>shared service;</li> <li>voluntary compact;</li> <li>jointly constituted authority;</li> <li>interface change;</li> <li>temporary pilot;</li> <li>legal or Treaty action;</li> <li>non-action;</li> <li>continued disagreement;</li> <li>a Constitutional Gap Notice.</li></ul> <h2>5.6 Authorization and acceptance</h2> <p>The institution holding the relevant competence authorizes action. Where several competences are involved, each affected authority authorizes the part within its domain unless a valid joint procedure exists.</p> <p>A recipient’s acceptance of a handoff establishes custody only to the extent recorded. It does not erase sender duties, unresolved dependencies, or the need for separate operational activation.</p> <h2>5.7 Implementation</h2> <p>Implementation responsibilities, resources, safety, reporting, action clocks, and termination shall be explicit. Meta-Governance may coordinate implementation only to the extent delegated.</p> <p>A capacity-limited authority should identify the support, shared service, temporary delegation, or lawful redesign needed to discharge its duty. Incapacity is not a constitutional gap and does not by itself justify permanent centralization.</p> <h2>5.8 Evaluation and revision</h2> <p>Evaluation shall include:</p> <ul><li>independent outcomes;</li> <li>subgroup and rights effects;</li> <li>coordination-added latency;</li> <li>administrative and translation burden;</li> <li>response and performance omissions;</li> <li>common-mode dependencies;</li> <li>unintended consequences;</li> <li>whether the action altered the causal condition;</li> <li>whether the same result could be obtained through less coordination;</li> <li>evidence that would falsify the intervention’s theory.</li></ul> <h2>5.9 Closure, devolution, and unresolved duties</h2> <p>The arrangement closes, narrows, devolves, or changes form when:</p> <ul><li>the condition ends;</li> <li>the delegated task is complete;</li> <li>participants withdraw under the charter;</li> <li>the intervention fails its review;</li> <li>a permanent lawful institution assumes the function;</li> <li>coordination is no longer necessary;</li> <li>a simpler or less intrusive mechanism becomes adequate.</li></ul> <p>Closure shall assign unresolved duties, records, remedies, contracts, workers, assets, data, and continuing service responsibilities. A legal sunset without material capacity to end is not complete closure.</p> <h1>6. Decision observability and epistemic governance</h1> <h2>6.1 Consequential decisions</h2> <p>A decision is consequential where it may materially affect rights, jurisdiction, public funds, Indigenous consent, ecological limits, access to essential services, sanctions, institutional continuity, or cross-boundary obligations.</p> <p>Consequential decisions require the records in this section.</p> <h2>6.2 Observation Contract</h2> <p>An Observation Contract states:</p> <ul><li>decision and authority;</li> <li>state or condition being estimated;</li> <li>observation channels;</li> <li>source provenance;</li> <li>uncertainty;</li> <li>missing populations;</li> <li>independence and correlation;</li> <li>retention and access;</li> <li>challenge and correction;</li> <li>review and retirement of the observation method.</li></ul> <h2>6.3 State Estimate Record</h2> <p>A State Estimate Record states:</p> <ul><li>competing hypotheses;</li> <li>supporting and conflicting evidence;</li> <li>confidence and unresolved ambiguity;</li> <li>distributional and rights implications;</li> <li>local and affected-community interpretations;</li> <li>proposed action and alternatives;</li> <li>evidence that would change the estimate.</li></ul> <h2>6.4 Observation Independence and Common-Mode Map</h2> <p>The map discloses shared observers, platforms, funders, incentives, algorithms, governance bodies, data pipelines, professional networks, methodological assumptions, and infrastructure.</p> <p>Several channels sharing one dependency shall not be counted as independent merely because their outputs are displayed separately.</p> <p>For high-consequence coordination, the map shall identify:</p> <ul><li>common points of failure;</li> <li>at least one operationally independent channel where feasible;</li> <li>fallback when shared infrastructure fails;</li> <li>residual correlation risk;</li> <li>the authority accepting that risk where genuine independence is impossible.</li></ul> <h2>6.5 Epistemic rights</h2> <p>Affected persons and institutions have rights to:</p> <ul><li>notice;</li> <li>inspect the evidence used where lawful;</li> <li>correct errors;</li> <li>provide context and counter-evidence;</li> <li>challenge standing and method;</li> <li>request human review;</li> <li>appeal consequential decisions;</li> <li>withhold protected information where no lawful override exists;</li> <li>obtain reversal or remediation when the decision fails.</li></ul> <h2>6.6 Protected non-transfer</h2> <p>A coordination interface may accept a bounded conclusion, condition, refusal, veto, or attestation without requiring disclosure of the knowledge behind it.</p> <p>Sacred, Indigenous-restricted, personal, security-sensitive, or intentionally unrecorded knowledge shall not be made open merely to satisfy a generalized transparency norm.</p> <h2>6.7 Minimum necessary documentation</h2> <p>Meta-Governance shall collect and retain only the records necessary for authority, coordination, accountability, learning, challenge, remedy, and closure.</p> <p>Documentation shall be accessible in plain-language, oral, visual, assisted, paper, or offline forms where required. Existing valid records shall be referenced rather than recreated. Records shall expire, archive, return, or delete according to purpose and law.</p> <p>The absence of a standardized field does not establish absence of value, standing, or relevant knowledge.</p> <h2>6.8 Federated institutional memory</h2> <p>Institutional memory should be federated, versioned, and purpose-limited.</p> <p>A public constitutional record may coexist with local archives, protected repositories, oral custody, and records that expire or are withdrawn. No universal blockchain or central repository is required.</p> <h1>7. Learning and Adaptation Profile</h1> <h2>7.1 From scalar velocity to a profile</h2> <p>v1.4 proposed a Learning Velocity Index comparing growth in observational dimensionality with growth in disturbance dimensionality. That intuition survives only as a research hypothesis.</p> <p>Institutional learning cannot be reduced to sensor expansion. v1.5 introduced a multidimensional <strong>Learning and Adaptation Profile</strong>; v1.6 retains it and adds coordination-added latency, administrative burden, response ownership, and structural reduction.</p> <h2>7.2 Profile dimensions</h2> <p>The profile reports separately:</p> <ol><li><strong>Detection latency</strong> — how quickly meaningful changes are noticed.</li> <li><strong>Coverage and missingness</strong> — who and what remain unseen.</li> <li><strong>Observer independence</strong> — whether distinct channels have distinct dependencies.</li> <li><strong>Uncertainty calibration</strong> — whether confidence matches later outcomes.</li> <li><strong>Challenge responsiveness</strong> — whether dissent changes inquiry or decisions.</li> <li><strong>Correction latency</strong> — how quickly known errors are repaired.</li> <li><strong>Implementation capacity</strong> — whether authorized changes can be carried out.</li> <li><strong>Outcome learning</strong> — whether independent human and ecological outcomes improve.</li> <li><strong>Model revision</strong> — whether failed predictions alter assumptions.</li> <li><strong>Architectural adaptability</strong> — whether the institution can change roles, interfaces, or structure lawfully.</li> <li><strong>Rights preservation</strong> — whether learning processes avoid surveillance, retaliation, and coercive legibility.</li> <li><strong>Knowledge diversity</strong> — whether relevant ways of knowing can participate without forced translation.</li> <li><strong>Coordination-added latency</strong> — delay introduced by interfaces, serial handoffs, and review architecture.</li> <li><strong>Administrative burden</strong> — time, expertise, translation, and documentation costs imposed on participants.</li> <li><strong>Response ownership</strong> — whether acceptance, refusal, delay, incapacity, and unresolved duties remain attributable.</li> <li><strong>Structural reduction</strong> — whether coordination is avoided, simplified, devolved, federated, or ended when no longer necessary.</li></ol> <p>No target composite is required.</p> <h2>7.3 Learning deficit review</h2> <p>A persistent deficit in one or more dimensions may trigger:</p> <ul><li>public notice;</li> <li>independent review;</li> <li>capacity support;</li> <li>an adversarial assessment;</li> <li>a causal-responsibility review;</li> <li>a response, refusal, and omission record;</li> <li>a bounded pilot proposal;</li> <li>a constitutional review request to the relevant authority.</li></ul> <p>It does not automatically shift resources or authority. A coordination body shall not use a deficit it helped define as a self-justifying claim to additional jurisdiction or budget.</p> <h2>7.4 Sensor-change governance</h2> <p>Adding, removing, or combining consequential observation channels requires review of:</p> <ul><li>purpose;</li> <li>marginal information value;</li> <li>surveillance and chilling effects;</li> <li>source dependence;</li> <li>cultural intrusion;</li> <li>cost and administrative burden;</li> <li>retirement and deletion.</li></ul> <h2>7.5 Learning through restraint</h2> <p>A system may learn by deciding not to observe, not to standardize, not to integrate, or not to act where the expected informational or rights cost exceeds the coordination benefit.</p> <h1>8. Variety Gap Assessment</h1> <h2>8.1 Purpose</h2> <p>A Variety Gap Assessment asks whether an institution’s legitimate action and observation capacities are adequate for a defined class of disturbances.</p> <p>It does not claim to calculate the total dimensionality of society or establish one objective measure of requisite variety.</p> <h2>8.2 Decision-specific scope</h2> <p>Each assessment identifies:</p> <ul><li>institution and lawful function;</li> <li>disturbance class;</li> <li>relevant timescale;</li> <li>required distinctions;</li> <li>available actions;</li> <li>observation channels;</li> <li>known blind spots;</li> <li>principal causal variables and their legal and practical controllers;</li> <li>rights and resource constraints;</li> <li>burdens assigned to the institution;</li> <li>upstream and external duties;</li> <li>competing assessments;</li> <li>whether an Impossible Mandate or Pilot Notice is warranted.</li></ul> <h2>8.3 Plural assessment</h2> <p>At least two independent assessment teams should be used for high-consequence reviews, including affected-community or domain-authority participation where feasible.</p> <p>Disagreement is published.</p> <h2>8.4 Alert status</h2> <p>A widening variety gap is an alert indicating that the institution may need:</p> <ul><li>narrower mandate;</li> <li>additional local observers;</li> <li>improved interfaces;</li> <li>capacity support;</li> <li>decentralization;</li> <li>new response options;</li> <li>reduced complexity imposed by other institutions;</li> <li>a bounded experiment;</li> <li>an Impossible Mandate or Pilot Notice;</li> <li>lawful constitution of missing shared authority.</li></ul> <p>The assessment cannot declare an institution constitutionally invalid or assign responsibility to a body merely because consequences appear within its territory or programme.</p> <h2>8.5 The environment is not the only variable</h2> <p>A mismatch may arise because:</p> <ul><li>disturbances changed;</li> <li>the institution lost capacity;</li> <li>its mandate is incoherent;</li> <li>another institution created avoidable complexity;</li> <li>observation is correlated or distorted;</li> <li>the chosen objective is wrong;</li> <li>the system is attempting to govern what should remain ungoverned.</li></ul> <h1>9. Fractal Governance Bandwidth and response obligations</h1> <h2>9.1 Bandwidth as diagnostic, not jurisdiction</h2> <p>Different governance layers operate at different characteristic latencies. A local emergency service, regional infrastructure body, global strategic institution, and intergenerational council do not act on the same timescale.</p> <p>Response speed does not create authority. Retaining authority may nevertheless carry a separately constituted duty to respond within a period appropriate to the condition.</p> <h2>9.2 Response-Time and Bandwidth Record</h2> <p>A consequential coordination arrangement shall document or reference:</p> <ul><li>disturbance timescale;</li> <li>sensing latency;</li> <li>validation latency;</li> <li>deliberation latency;</li> <li>acknowledgment clock;</li> <li>decision clock;</li> <li>implementation or action clock;</li> <li>feedback latency;</li> <li>coordination-added latency;</li> <li>ecological or social lag;</li> <li>risk of acting too slowly;</li> <li>risk of acting before diagnosis;</li> <li>lawful clock source;</li> <li>interim protection or pre-authorized safe state;</li> <li>escalation when a clock is missed.</li></ul> <p>A response clock should cite valid law, charter, compact, contract, voluntary commitment, a GMEAIA-07 assessment, or a combination of these. The record does not create a legal duty where none exists.</p> <h2>9.3 Response and performance omission</h2> <p>The lifecycle shall distinguish:</p> <ul><li>no response to a valid request;</li> <li>lawful refusal;</li> <li>justified deferment;</li> <li>misrouting;</li> <li>practical capacity limitation;</li> <li>constitutional absence;</li> <li>acceptance followed by missed action or review clocks.</li></ul> <p>Acceptance does not close the omission question if the recipient then fails to perform the accepted duty.</p> <h2>9.4 Non-override rule</h2> <p>A higher-scale body may not override a lower-scale decision merely because it operates on a longer planning horizon or claims a broader model.</p> <p>Intervention requires:</p> <ul><li>valid jurisdiction;</li> <li>demonstrated cross-boundary or rights condition;</li> <li>minimum necessary authority;</li> <li>causal-responsibility analysis;</li> <li>local sensemaking;</li> <li>protected continuity;</li> <li>review and return.</li></ul> <h2>9.5 Fast signals and slow variables</h2> <p>Fast operational telemetry shall not automatically control slow constitutional or ecological decisions. Slow-variable changes require the specialized protocols of the relevant domain frameworks.</p> <h1>10. Bypass Architecture Protocol</h1> <h2>10.1 Purpose</h2> <p>A bypass is a bounded institutional experiment created where an existing system cannot presently perform a necessary function or test a credible alternative.</p> <p>A bypass is not proof that the incumbent has lost legitimacy or that the experiment deserves permanent authority. A bypass shall be small enough for safety and large enough to expose the causal mechanism it claims to test.</p> <h2>10.2 Authorization</h2> <p>A Bypass Charter must be authorized by the institution holding the relevant competence or by a valid Treaty procedure.</p> <p>Meta-Governance may incubate and coordinate the proposal but may not charter coercive jurisdiction on its own.</p> <h2>10.3 Required charter terms</h2> <p>The charter states:</p> <ol><li>problem and affected population;</li> <li>authority and jurisdiction;</li> <li>participants and non-participants;</li> <li>rights and protected minimums;</li> <li>resources and funding;</li> <li>observation and evaluation;</li> <li>data boundaries;</li> <li>interaction with incumbent institutions;</li> <li>duration and review;</li> <li>exit and closure;</li> <li>minimum causally adequate scope and conclusions the experiment cannot establish;</li> <li>response duties and clocks for consequential incumbent handoffs;</li> <li>possible future dispositions;</li> <li>continuity obligations;</li> <li>ordinary owner, maintenance capacity, and support-withdrawal test if continuation is proposed.</li></ol> <h2>10.4 Possible dispositions</h2> <p>A bypass may:</p> <ul><li>dissolve after the experiment;</li> <li>be reintegrated into an incumbent;</li> <li>become a federated partner;</li> <li>continue in stable coexistence;</li> <li>transfer a bounded function through lawful agreement;</li> <li>contribute to a newly constituted successor.</li></ul> <p>There is no presumption that reintegration is always correct or that parallel existence is always fragmentation.</p> <h2>10.5 Learning transfer</h2> <p>A bypass should publish transferable findings where lawful, including failures and contextual limits. Protected information remains protected.</p> <p>The incumbent is not compelled to adopt a result merely because the bypass reports success. Where a response duty is validly constituted, non-adoption, deferment, capacity limitation, or referral must receive a reasoned and reviewable response. Timeout does not transfer the function to the bypass.</p> <h2>10.6 Anti-enclave safeguards</h2> <p>A bypass must be reviewed for:</p> <ul><li>privileged access;</li> <li>displacement of public obligations;</li> <li>extraction of data or resources;</li> <li>exclusion of affected populations;</li> <li>regulatory arbitrage;</li> <li>dependency on unaccountable funders;</li> <li>irreversible infrastructure or rights effects.</li></ul> <h1>11. Coexistence, integration, and succession</h1> <h2>11.1 Three relationship tracks</h2> <p>Meta-Governance may support:</p> <ul><li><strong>reform partnership</strong> within existing institutions;</li> <li><strong>parallel experimentation or service provision</strong>;</li> <li><strong>lawful succession</strong> where a valid authority transfers or reconstitutes a function.</li></ul> <p>The tracks are options, not a predetermined evolutionary sequence.</p> <h2>11.2 Non-predatory transition</h2> <p>New institutions should not manufacture incumbent failure, withhold essential cooperation, or use information advantages to force transfer.</p> <p>Incumbents should not sabotage lawful alternatives solely to preserve institutional position.</p> <h2>11.3 Functional transfer</h2> <p>A transfer requires:</p> <ul><li>authority from the body legally able to transfer the function;</li> <li>authority-formation classification;</li> <li>public reasons;</li> <li>affected-party participation;</li> <li>a Causal Responsibility Profile;</li> <li>capability, dependency, and common-mode risk assessment;</li> <li>rights and continuity plan;</li> <li>assets, liabilities, records, and staff treatment;</li> <li>response and handoff records;</li> <li>appeal and review;</li> <li>conditions for return or further change.</li></ul> <p>An Accountable Coordination Audit or Variety Gap Assessment may support the case but cannot execute the transfer.</p> <h2>11.4 Stable coexistence</h2> <p>Two institutions may legitimately continue in parallel where they:</p> <ul><li>serve different populations;</li> <li>use different epistemic or cultural approaches;</li> <li>provide redundancy;</li> <li>allow meaningful choice;</li> <li>do not create destructive externalities;</li> <li>maintain interoperable minimums where necessary.</li></ul> <h2>11.5 Immune-war de-escalation</h2> <p>Transition actors should distinguish:</p> <ul><li>legitimate legal resistance;</li> <li>self-protective institutional obstruction;</li> <li>rights-based refusal;</li> <li>misinformation;</li> <li>ordinary political disagreement.</li></ul> <p>No AI system or coordination council may classify political opposition as an “immune response” and thereby justify exceptional power.</p> <h1>12. Institutional sunset and dissolution</h1> <h2>12.1 Sunset review</h2> <p>Every Meta-Governance institution shall have periodic review and, where appropriate, a sunset date.</p> <p>Review may consider:</p> <ul><li>mission completion;</li> <li>continued need;</li> <li>rights effects;</li> <li>cost;</li> <li>duplication;</li> <li>capture;</li> <li>trust;</li> <li>adaptability;</li> <li>budget, staffing, prestige, appointment, and survival incentives;</li> <li>whether the institution benefits from identifying additional coordination need;</li> <li>whether a less intrusive arrangement is available;</li> <li>whether the function can be avoided, simplified, devolved, federated, or ended.</li></ul> <h2>12.2 Governance Sunset Commission</h2> <p>A Governance Sunset Commission may conduct independent reviews and recommend continuation, reform, devolution, merger, federation, or dissolution.</p> <p>It shall not unilaterally dissolve institutions unless a constitution or charter expressly grants that narrow power and supplies appeal, continuity, and review.</p> <h2>12.3 Appointment and independence</h2> <p>Commission members should have:</p> <ul><li>fixed, non-renewable or tightly limited terms;</li> <li>conflict restrictions;</li> <li>no simultaneous leadership in reviewed institutions;</li> <li>transparent methods;</li> <li>dissent rights;</li> <li>removal only for defined cause.</li></ul> <p>Appointment by a foresight body alone is insufficient for institutions with constitutional consequences.</p> <h2>12.4 Dissolution authorization</h2> <p>Dissolution is authorized by:</p> <ul><li>the constituting authority;</li> <li>a procedure in the institution’s charter;</li> <li>a competent tribunal;</li> <li>a Treaty process validly empowered to act.</li></ul> <h2>12.5 Continuity plan</h2> <p>Before dissolution, publish:</p> <ul><li>rights and service continuity;</li> <li>records and protected knowledge;</li> <li>staff, pension, contractor, and contractual obligations;</li> <li>funding for transition and closure work;</li> <li>assets and liabilities;</li> <li>active cases, remedies, and appeals;</li> <li>transfer or termination of powers;</li> <li>data return, deletion, archive, and access revocation;</li> <li>public communication;</li> <li>post-dissolution accountability.</li></ul> <p>A sunset is not materially complete while staff, beneficiaries, records, essential services, or unresolved duties lack a lawful destination.</p> <h2>12.6 Protocol preservation</h2> <p>Useful protocols and knowledge may survive institutional dissolution, but preservation must respect consent, data retention, Indigenous knowledge, security, and the right to deletion or non-transfer.</p> <h1>13. Emergency coordination</h1> <h2>13.1 Constitutional basis</h2> <p>Meta-Governance emergency coordination operates under Treaty’s Emergency Constitutionalism provisions and relevant domain protocols.</p> <p>A coordination council may activate a <strong>convening function</strong> within 24 hours. Convening is not command authority.</p> <h2>13.2 Emergency Coordination Record</h2> <p>The record states:</p> <ul><li>emergency condition and evidence;</li> <li>declaring authority;</li> <li>participating institutions;</li> <li>powers activated;</li> <li>powers prohibited;</li> <li>affected area and population;</li> <li>Indigenous and local interfaces;</li> <li>essential services and material minimums;</li> <li>data and observation rules;</li> <li>duration, review, and termination;</li> <li>restoration and after-action obligations.</li></ul> <h2>13.3 Provisional action</h2> <p>Where immediate action is lawfully authorized, provisional measures remain narrow, reversible, evidence-preserving, and normally limited to the Treaty’s 72-hour window before fuller authorization.</p> <h2>13.4 No emergency competence creep</h2> <p>Emergency coordination shall not be used to:</p> <ul><li>create permanent jurisdiction;</li> <li>bypass Indigenous consent unrelated to the immediate necessity;</li> <li>compel work outside Work in Liberation safeguards;</li> <li>alter financial architecture permanently;</li> <li>suppress dissent or system-canary testimony;</li> <li>retain emergency data without review;</li> <li>normalize repeated temporary authority.</li></ul> <h2>13.5 Crisis work and finance</h2> <p>Mobilization of people and Community Providers follows Work in Liberation. Financial liquidity and settlement follow Financial Systems. AUBI Layer 1 and protected minimums remain governed by AUBI and the Treaty.</p> <h2>13.6 After-action review</h2> <p>Review shall test:</p> <ul><li>whether the declaration was justified;</li> <li>whose evidence was missing;</li> <li>rights and subgroup effects;</li> <li>whether provisional measures became de facto policy;</li> <li>whether ordinary capacity should be strengthened;</li> <li>what data should be deleted;</li> <li>what compensation or repair is owed.</li></ul> <h1>14. Power, participation, and cognitive equity</h1> <h2>14.1 Multi-Modal Deliberation Protocol</h2> <p>Official Meta-Governance proceedings shall provide appropriate combinations of:</p> <ul><li>real-time verbal deliberation;</li> <li>asynchronous written participation;</li> <li>oral and narrative testimony;</li> <li>visual and systems-mapping methods;</li> <li>supported decision-making;</li> <li>interpretation and translation;</li> <li>sensory-accessible and low-stimulation formats;</li> <li>offline participation.</li></ul> <p>Equal formal availability is not enough; the process shall examine whether one mode still controls agenda, prestige, or decision weight.</p> <p>Participation design does not itself allocate constitutional seats. Where deliberative participation feeds a constituted council, the relationship between voice, recommendation, vote, veto, and delegated authority shall be explicit under §3.7.</p> <h2>14.2 Facilitator role</h2> <p>Facilitators shall be trained in:</p> <ul><li>power-aware process;</li> <li>cognitive and disability inclusion;</li> <li>cross-cultural humility;</li> <li>conflict of interest;</li> <li>distinction between mediation and adjudication;</li> <li>protection of dissent;</li> <li>documentation of unresolved disagreement.</li></ul> <p>Facilitators do not certify substantive truth or cultural legitimacy.</p> <h2>14.3 Conscious Refuser protections</h2> <p>A participant may refuse a process that:</p> <ul><li>lacks authority;</li> <li>demands ethical compromise;</li> <li>requires disclosure of protected knowledge;</li> <li>imposes coercive legibility;</li> <li>fails the Accountable Coordination test;</li> <li>creates inaccessible or cognitively harmful conditions.</li></ul> <p>Refusal shall not automatically reduce standing, benefits, future participation, or civic status.</p> <h2>14.4 System Sanity and Cognitive Friction Audits</h2> <p>Audits may examine:</p> <ul><li>unnecessary cognitive load;</li> <li>contradictory rules;</li> <li>procedural theatre;</li> <li>moral injury;</li> <li>sensory exclusion;</li> <li>excessive documentation;</li> <li>metric gaming;</li> <li>demands to affirm institutional narratives contradicted by experience.</li></ul> <p>An audit is not a mental-health diagnosis of participants.</p> <h2>14.5 Narrative–reality alignment</h2> <p>Institutions shall compare public claims with observable outcomes, complaints, distributional effects, and independent evidence.</p> <p>The goal is not a single official narrative but visibility into where narratives diverge from conditions.</p> <h1>15. Knowledge and technology</h1> <h2>15.1 Human authority supremacy</h2> <p>Humans and legitimate institutions remain responsible for ethical and constitutional judgment. An AI recommendation does not dilute accountability.</p> <h2>15.2 Permitted AI support</h2> <p>AI may support:</p> <ul><li>translation;</li> <li>document comparison;</li> <li>scenario generation;</li> <li>anomaly detection;</li> <li>simulation;</li> <li>accessibility;</li> <li>retrieval and summarization;</li> <li>administrative routing.</li></ul> <h2>15.3 Prohibited AI functions</h2> <p>AI may not independently determine:</p> <ul><li>jurisdiction;</li> <li>Indigenous identity or authority;</li> <li>consent or FPIC;</li> <li>truth of sacred or cultural claims;</li> <li>criminal liability;</li> <li>sanctions;</li> <li>emergency declaration;</li> <li>institutional capture;</li> <li>authority transfer;</li> <li>dissolution.</li></ul> <h2>15.4 Distributed sensemaking</h2> <p>Distributed Sensemaking Councils or networks may connect scientific, local, professional, Indigenous-authorized, experiential, and civil-society knowledge.</p> <p>They shall document translation loss and preserve the option that knowledge systems remain partially incommensurable.</p> <h2>15.5 Cognitive immunity</h2> <p>Information-integrity measures should strengthen source evaluation, public reasoning, media literacy, provenance, and plural inquiry.</p> <p>They shall not create a centralized authority over legitimate political belief or treat criticism of the GGF as disinformation.</p> <h2>15.6 Technology refusal and community veto</h2> <p>Communities may reject intrusive or culturally inappropriate technology within their jurisdiction, subject to valid rights and cross-boundary obligations. Indigenous technology decisions follow Indigenous Sovereignty and Planetary Healing.</p> <h1>16. Funding and resource sharing</h1> <h2>16.1 No self-authorized tax</h2> <p>Meta-Governance v1.6 does not create a Coordination Infrastructure Tax or determine a global levy rate.</p> <p>Funding may come from:</p> <ul><li>Treaty-authorized revenue protocols;</li> <li>assessed contributions;</li> <li>voluntary contributions;</li> <li>grants subject to independence safeguards;</li> <li>shared-service fees under compacts;</li> <li>time-limited pilot funding.</li></ul> <h2>16.2 Funding record</h2> <p>Every Meta-Governance institution shall disclose:</p> <ul><li>sources;</li> <li>restrictions;</li> <li>allocation authority;</li> <li>conflicts of interest;</li> <li>administrative overhead;</li> <li>incentives linked to case volume, coordination activity, compliance, renewal, or expansion;</li> <li>equity provisions;</li> <li>support for devolution, closure, staff transition, and local capacity transfer;</li> <li>audit and termination conditions.</li></ul> <h2>16.3 Equity participation</h2> <p>Participation support may include compensation, care, travel, accessibility, translation, connectivity, and community-controlled research resources.</p> <p>Fixed global allocation percentages are proposals requiring lawful budget authorization, not constitutional rules of this framework.</p> <h2>16.4 No automatic reallocation</h2> <p>A capture alert may trigger review or a temporary hold where lawfully authorized. It may not automatically redirect funds to the body issuing the alert.</p> <h2>16.5 Resource-sharing interfaces</h2> <p>Shared infrastructure and crisis resources shall use explicit compacts defining ownership, access, priority, liability, maintenance, exit, data rights, switching costs, fallback, and the material feasibility of withdrawal. Technical control shall not become political authority through pricing, standards, or access decisions.</p> <h1>17. Capture resistance, common-mode risk, and objective reproduction</h1> <h2>17.1 Structural rather than rhetorical protection</h2> <p>A body is not capture-resistant because it uses rotation, AI detection, sortition, transparency, or the word “polycentric.” Capture resistance requires independent powers, practical challenge, exit, rights, limits on self-expansion, and visibility into the incentives that reproduce its behavior.</p> <h2>17.2 Capture and objective-reproduction indicators</h2> <p>Possible indicators include:</p> <ul><li>self-justifying mandate expansion;</li> <li>asymmetric transparency;</li> <li>prohibitive or merely formal exit;</li> <li>concentration of agenda control;</li> <li>correlated observers and common infrastructure;</li> <li>retaliation against dissent;</li> <li>funding dependency;</li> <li>selective enforcement;</li> <li>permanent emergency;</li> <li>metrics optimized for institutional survival;</li> <li>procedural throughput treated as proof of value;</li> <li>case, meeting, audit, or record volume rewarded independently of outcomes;</li> <li>staff, funder, vendor, or facilitator dependence on continued coordination;</li> <li>routine renewal despite mission completion;</li> <li>closure costs borne by workers and communities while continuation benefits accrue to the institution.</li></ul> <p>No target veto frequency, participation rate, trust score, or decision-share percentage proves health or capture by itself.</p> <h2>17.3 Independence and Objective-Reproduction Review</h2> <p>Long-lived, systemically central, or self-renewing Meta-Governance mechanisms shall complete or reference the applicable GMEAIA review. It shall examine:</p> <ul><li>shared funders, data, AI, vendors, infrastructure, methods, professional networks, and ontologies;</li> <li>common points of failure and operational fallback;</li> <li>budget, appointment, promotion, prestige, blame, and survival incentives;</li> <li>incentives of auditors, facilitators, tool stewards, and funders;</li> <li>behavior likely to regenerate after reform;</li> <li>conditions that make the coordination function unnecessary.</li></ul> <p>Organizational separation does not establish independence where the same substrate shapes all channels.</p> <h2>17.4 Integrity response ladder</h2> <p>A response may include:</p> <ol><li>notice and evidence preservation;</li> <li>independent inquiry;</li> <li>public response and correction;</li> <li>capacity support or conflict separation;</li> <li>temporary reversible containment where lawfully authorized;</li> <li>governance or incentive reform;</li> <li>lawful removal, transfer, devolution, or closure;</li> <li>dissolution through authorized process.</li></ol> <p>The institution issuing the alert should not control the full response chain.</p> <h2>17.5 Whistleblowers and dissent</h2> <p>Participants, staff, observers, and affected people shall have protected channels for confidential reporting, external review, public dissent, and remedy.</p> <h2>17.6 Integrity of the integrity system</h2> <p>Auditors, Sunset Commissions, Adversary bodies, foresight units, and review panels require their own:</p> <ul><li>mandate limits;</li> <li>funding and incentive disclosure;</li> <li>source-independence and common-mode maps;</li> <li>appeal;</li> <li>rotation or term rules;</li> <li>external audit;</li> <li>response duties where validly constituted;</li> <li>dissolution pathways.</li></ul> <p>No exclusive conformance certifier is created by this framework. Conformance may be supported through self-attestation with evidence, peer review, independent challenge, and lawful audit. A body reviewing conformance should be institutionally separate from funding decisions where feasible.</p> <h1>18. Evaluation framework</h1> <h2>18.1 No single Meta-Governance score</h2> <p>The framework does not use one composite index to certify a governance system as healthy, adaptive, legitimate, or future-ready.</p> <h2>18.2 Evaluation dimensions</h2> <p>Report separately:</p> <h3>Coordination effectiveness</h3> <ul><li>whether the relevant authorities were connected;</li> <li>whether causal responsibility and duty-holders were correctly identified;</li> <li>commitment completion;</li> <li>acceptance, refusal, timeout, capacity-limit, and performance-omission patterns;</li> <li>unresolved interface and constitutional gaps;</li> <li>coordination-added latency;</li> <li>duplication, translation, and administrative load;</li> <li>outcome latency and independent effects;</li> <li>coordination avoided, simplified, devolved, federated, or terminated.</li></ul> <h3>Legitimacy and participation</h3> <ul><li>standing of affected groups;</li> <li>accessibility;</li> <li>power distribution;</li> <li>refusal without retaliation;</li> <li>appeal and correction;</li> <li>missing participants.</li></ul> <h3>Epistemic quality</h3> <ul><li>source and infrastructure independence;</li> <li>common-mode risk and fallback;</li> <li>uncertainty calibration;</li> <li>visibility of disagreement;</li> <li>local and Indigenous knowledge boundaries;</li> <li>model revision after disconfirmation.</li></ul> <h3>Adaptability</h3> <ul><li>correction latency;</li> <li>capacity support;</li> <li>lawful architectural change;</li> <li>pilot learning;</li> <li>ability to de-escalate or close coordination.</li></ul> <h3>Rights and equity</h3> <ul><li>subgroup effects;</li> <li>privacy and protected non-transfer;</li> <li>economic and administrative burden;</li> <li>disability and cognitive access;</li> <li>retaliation or coercion.</li></ul> <h3>Planetary and intergenerational outcomes</h3> <p>Use domain-authorized ecological and future-generations evidence. Meta-Governance does not create its own universal ecological score.</p> <h3>Capture resistance</h3> <ul><li>mandate discipline;</li> <li>symmetric accountability;</li> <li>exit and challenge;</li> <li>independent authorization;</li> <li>institutional self-limitation.</li></ul> <h3>Structural impermanence</h3> <ul><li>completed and materially supported sunsets;</li> <li>successful devolution and local capacity transfer;</li> <li>continuity during closure;</li> <li>unresolved-duty custody;</li> <li>reduction of unnecessary coordination infrastructure;</li> <li>proportion of temporary bodies routinely renewed;</li> <li>whether success makes the coordination layer less necessary.</li></ul> <h2>18.3 Real-time dashboards</h2> <p>Dashboards may show operational status, uncertainty, clocks, handoff states, and alerts. They shall not make constitutional judgments, infer legitimacy from conformance, or expose protected personal or community information.</p> <p>Throughput indicators—meetings, records, interfaces, referrals, or audits—shall not be reported as success without independent outcomes and administrative-burden context.</p> <h2>18.4 Citizen Review Panels</h2> <p>Citizen panels may require a public response, commission review, or exercise binding authority only where the constituting instrument grants it. “Community trust” must not be inferred from platform participation alone.</p> <h2>18.5 Framework Falsification Register</h2> <p>Meta-Governance v1.6 advances the following framework-level hypotheses:</p> <ol><li>bounded coordination reduces unresolved cross-domain conflict, duplication, and responsibility gaps without producing residual sovereignty;</li> <li>decision-chain separation reduces concentration without creating unacceptable delay or omission;</li> <li>plural observation and common-mode review improve calibration and correction;</li> <li>bypasses transfer valid learning without becoming privileged enclaves;</li> <li>sunset, devolution, and closure occur materially rather than only legally;</li> <li>causal-responsibility and response records reduce burden dumping and unowned non-action;</li> <li>proportional documentation improves accountability without excluding lower-capacity participants.</li></ol> <p>Evidence supporting narrowing, suspension, replacement, or non-scaling includes:</p> <ul><li>coordination-added latency or administrative burden exceeding the harm reduced;</li> <li>persistent constitutional gaps or timeouts despite correct process;</li> <li>coordination bodies expanding budget, staff, data, or mandate without independent outcome improvement;</li> <li>nominally plural observers sharing one decisive substrate;</li> <li>bypasses succeeding only under exceptional support or failing at ordinary institutional transition;</li> <li>sunsets producing routine renewal or abandonment of workers, services, records, or remedies;</li> <li>simpler direct or domain-specific arrangements performing better;</li> <li>low-capacity actors being systematically excluded by the documentation architecture.</li></ul> <p>No single indicator decides the framework’s validity. Review shall publish evidence, disagreement, and the proposed scope of any narrowing or replacement.</p> <h1>19. Implementation pathway</h1> <h2>19.1 Phase 0 — Constitutional conformance</h2> <p>Before operational expansion:</p> <ul><li>align charters with the Treaty and Constitutional Interface Specification;</li> <li>adopt compatible GMEAIA authority-effect, consequence-profile, handoff, clock, and closure semantics;</li> <li>identify retained, delegated, transferred, concurrent, jointly constituted, and absent authority;</li> <li>remove automatic authority-transfer and dissolution triggers;</li> <li>classify existing composition formulas as either enacted by a valid constituting instrument or illustrative design profiles under §3.7;</li> <li>remove or suspend any framework-level rule that prescribes the composition of another independently constituted body without that body’s own adoption;</li> <li>replace GGF-imposed Indigenous quotas or proxy-selection routes with interfaces to the relevant peoples’ own authority under §3.8;</li> <li>establish Indigenous interfaces through the applicable framework and Indigenous authority;</li> <li>create Observation Contract, State Estimate, Causal Responsibility, and response-record profiles;</li> <li>define proportional documentation, appeal, remedy, and material closure.</li></ul> <h2>19.2 Phase 1 — Interface pilots</h2> <p>Pilot low-coercion functions such as:</p> <ul><li>cross-domain mapping;</li> <li>shared-service coordination;</li> <li>multi-modal deliberation;</li> <li>plural state estimates;</li> <li>federated institutional memory;</li> <li>voluntary foresight networks;</li> <li>sunset review of temporary working groups;</li> <li>response, refusal, and omission tracking;</li> <li>minimum-necessary documentation and low-tech record equivalence.</li></ul> <h2>19.3 Phase 2 — Bounded adaptive pilots</h2> <p>Test:</p> <ul><li>Learning and Adaptation Profiles;</li> <li>Variety Gap Assessments;</li> <li>Bypass Charters;</li> <li>polycentric interface records;</li> <li>stable-coexistence and federation options;</li> <li>independent integrity and common-mode reviews;</li> <li>minimum causally adequate pilots;</li> <li>material closure and support-withdrawal tests.</li></ul> <p>No pilot should begin with compulsory taxation, sanction, emergency command, or authority transfer.</p> <h2>19.4 Phase 3 — Treaty activation</h2> <p>Where Treaty institutions lawfully enter into force, Meta-Governance functions may be activated through explicit charters and budgets.</p> <h2>19.5 Phase 4 — Review, devolution, and succession</h2> <p>The implementation programme must evaluate not only expansion but:</p> <ul><li>which coordination functions can return to domain institutions;</li> <li>which shared services can be federated;</li> <li>which bodies should close;</li> <li>which experiments should remain plural;</li> <li>whether the Meta-Governance layer is becoming less necessary;</li> <li>whether coordination-added latency and administrative burden justify redesign;</li> <li>whether the framework-level falsification findings require narrowing or replacement.</li></ul> <h2>19.6 No fixed civilizational timetable</h2> <p>The previous 15-year plan is retained only as an illustrative planning horizon. Institutional readiness depends on legitimacy, capacity, consent, and outcomes rather than calendar progression.</p> <h1>20. Framework interfaces</h1> <h2>20.1 Treaty for Our Only Home</h2> <p>The Treaty supplies:</p> <ul><li>constitutional authority;</li> <li>institutions;</li> <li>decision classes;</li> <li>emergency rules;</li> <li>finance and enforcement protocols;</li> <li>entry into force;</li> <li>amendment and withdrawal.</li></ul> <p>Meta-Governance supplies coordination procedures inside those limits.</p> <h2>20.2 Constitutional Interface Specification</h2> <p>The Specification supplies shared rules for:</p> <ul><li>plural legitimacy;</li> <li>causal subsidiarity and responsibility;</li> <li>duties attached to retained jurisdiction;</li> <li>omission, incapacity, and constitutional gaps;</li> <li>proportional procedural burden;</li> <li>observation;</li> <li>automatic controls;</li> <li>veto and injunction;</li> <li>bypasses;</li> <li>transfer;</li> <li>dissolution;</li> <li>continuity;</li> <li>framework-level falsification.</li></ul> <p>Meta-Governance applies those constitutional boundaries to coordination-body formation, representation functions, composition status, and institutional selection under §3.1 and §3.7. It does not convert illustrative participation goals into constitutional seat entitlements.</p> <h2>20.3 GMEAIA</h2> <p>GMEAIA supplies the operational lifecycle grammar for:</p> <ul><li>authority effects and formation;</li> <li>consequence profiles;</li> <li>causal-responsibility records;</li> <li>acknowledgment, decision, and action clocks;</li> <li>accepted, refused, deferred, referred, timed-out, capacity-limited, and constitutional-gap states;</li> <li>sender and recipient custody;</li> <li>cross-framework handoffs;</li> <li>proportional record bundles;</li> <li>correction, unresolved duties, and closure.</li></ul> <p>Meta-Governance does not redefine those controlled states locally.</p> <h2>20.4 Indigenous Sovereignty and Planetary Healing</h2> <p>That framework governs the GGF’s obligations toward Indigenous sovereignty, consent, knowledge, technology, and community-determined institutions.</p> <p>Meta-Governance does not define an Indigenous representation percentage, manufacture a universal Indigenous electorate, appoint proxies for affected peoples, or create an Earth Council. A domain framework may identify the need for Indigenous authority or knowledge participation; the relevant peoples determine their representatives or other interface through their own valid processes.</p> <h2>20.5 Polycentric Governance Architecture</h2> <p>The Polycentric framework supplies useful horizontal interface concepts among territorial councils, Commons Trusts, practice institutions, and Indigenous authority.</p> <p>Meta-Governance applies those concepts without assuming universal chambers, target veto frequencies, automatic jury dissolution, generalized trust scores, or GGF-designed Indigenous institutions.</p> <h2>20.6 AUBI</h2> <p>AUBI supplies material security that makes refusal more credible and defines population-level observation, claimant rights, and protected unobservability.</p> <p>Meta-Governance may not use AUBI, LMCI, or Love Ledger data as generalized legitimacy, participation, or institutional-health scores.</p> <h2>20.7 Financial Systems</h2> <p>Financial Systems governs Hearts, Leaves, reserves, redemption, settlement, financial crisis controls, ICTL, and NARP.</p> <p>Meta-Governance may coordinate financial interfaces but does not set exchange rates, redirect funds automatically, or activate Sovereign Debt Transformation.</p> <h2>20.8 Nested Economies</h2> <p>Nested Economies governs economic subsidiarity, BAZ-to-BAZ interfaces, voluntary coordination layers, and solidarity protocols.</p> <h2>20.9 Work in Liberation</h2> <p>Work in Liberation governs worker rights, Community Providers, professional and guild institutions, work matching, commissioning, and emergency mobilisation.</p> <h2>20.10 Domain frameworks</h2> <p>Health, peace, technology, justice, climate, biodiversity, migration, education, and other domain frameworks retain substantive authority within their competence.</p> <h1>21. Conformance requirements</h1> <p>A Meta-Governance institution conforms to v1.6 only if it can show:</p> <ul><li>lawful constituting authority and authority-formation classification;</li> <li>bounded purpose and competence;</li> <li>a justified GMEAIA consequence profile;</li> <li>a Polycentric Interface Record where relevant;</li> <li>decision-chain separation;</li> <li>Causal Responsibility Profile for consequential coordination;</li> <li>Observation Contracts and plural State Estimate Records where required;</li> <li>Observation Independence and Common-Mode Maps;</li> <li>accessible participation and independent advice;</li> <li>representation functions, composition status, selector authority, term/vacancy/removal rules, and dispute routes where membership is consequential;</li> <li>no framework-imposed composition rule for an independently constituted external body without that body’s valid adoption;</li> <li>Indigenous recognition, consent, protected-knowledge safeguards, and no GGF-imposed Indigenous quota or proxy appointment;</li> <li>explicit separation of representation from consent and selection from jurisdiction;</li> <li>response-duty source, clocks, handoff states, and unresolved-duty custody where applicable;</li> <li>distinction among lawful refusal, deferment, timeout, capacity limitation, obstruction, and constitutional absence;</li> <li>limits on AI and automation;</li> <li>rights-bounded emergency procedures;</li> <li>minimum necessary and consequence-proportional documentation;</li> <li>appeal, correction, remedy, and restoration;</li> <li>funding and objective-reproduction transparency;</li> <li>sunset, material closure, and continuity provisions;</li> <li>independent outcome evidence;</li> <li>no inference that conformance establishes legitimacy or success;</li> <li>no automatic authority transfer, funding capture, sanction, or dissolution from its own metrics;</li> <li>a framework or mechanism-level falsification profile where long-lived or systemically central.</li></ul> <p>Conformance may be supported through evidence-backed self-attestation, peer review, independent challenge, and lawful audit. This framework creates no exclusive conformance certifier.</p> <p>Non-conformance shall be published with a remediation plan or explicit statement that the mechanism remains a non-operative proposal. <code>P0</code> constitutional conflicts, <code>P1</code> interface gaps, and <code>P2</code> operational gaps retain the meanings defined by the Constitutional Interface Specification and must not be confused with GMEAIA consequence profiles <code>CP0</code>–<code>CP3</code>.</p> <hr> <h1>Layer 3 — Operational templates</h1> <h1>Appendix A — Meta-Governance Charter Record</h1> <p><strong>Institution or arrangement:</strong><br> <strong>Constituting authority:</strong><br> <strong>Purpose:</strong><br> <strong>Decision class:</strong><br> <strong>Participating authorities:</strong><br> <strong>Affected non-participants:</strong><br> <strong>Representation functions:</strong> constituency / affectedness / expertise / delegated authority / protected interest / sortition / advisory<br> <strong>Composition status:</strong> enacted / illustrative design profile / not applicable<br> <strong>Constituting source for composition, if enacted:</strong><br> <strong>Authorized selector or selection process:</strong><br> <strong>Term / mandate expiry:</strong><br> <strong>Vacancy / replacement:</strong><br> <strong>Recall / removal:</strong><br> <strong>Selection or composition challenge route:</strong><br> <strong>Indigenous authority basis, where applicable:</strong><br> <strong>Authority formation:</strong> retained / delegated / transferred / concurrent / jointly constituted<br> <strong>Consequence profile:</strong> CP0 / CP1 / CP2 / CP3<br> <strong>Causal-responsibility record:</strong><br> <strong>Response-duty source and effect:</strong><br> <strong>Acknowledgment, decision, and action clocks:</strong><br> <strong>Delegated powers:</strong><br> <strong>Prohibited powers:</strong><br> <strong>Duration:</strong><br> <strong>Funding:</strong><br> <strong>Decision rule:</strong><br> <strong>Appeal:</strong><br> <strong>Review date:</strong><br> <strong>Closure conditions:</strong></p> <h1>Appendix B — Polycentric Interface Record</h1> <p><strong>Condition requiring coordination:</strong><br> <strong>Territorial authorities:</strong><br> <strong>Commons authorities:</strong><br> <strong>Practice/labour authorities:</strong><br> <strong>Indigenous authorities:</strong><br> <strong>Treaty or tribunal authorities:</strong><br> <strong>Authority retained by each:</strong><br> <strong>Authority delegated or jointly constituted:</strong><br> <strong>Principal causal variables and controllers:</strong><br> <strong>Burdens, resources, and continuing upstream duties:</strong><br> <strong>Response and handoff record:</strong><br> <strong>Information exchanged:</strong><br> <strong>Protected information:</strong><br> <strong>Disagreement procedure:</strong><br> <strong>Rights and minimums:</strong><br> <strong>Exit and restoration:</strong></p> <h1>Appendix C — Learning and Adaptation Profile</h1> <p>For each dimension, report evidence, uncertainty, subgroup differences, and trend without collapsing them into one score.</p> <ul><li>Detection latency:</li> <li>Coverage and missingness:</li> <li>Observer independence:</li> <li>Calibration:</li> <li>Challenge responsiveness:</li> <li>Correction latency:</li> <li>Implementation capacity:</li> <li>Outcome learning:</li> <li>Model revision:</li> <li>Architectural adaptability:</li> <li>Rights preservation:</li> <li>Knowledge diversity:</li> <li>Coordination-added latency:</li> <li>Administrative burden:</li> <li>Response ownership and omission:</li> <li>Structural reduction:</li></ul> <h1>Appendix D — Variety Gap Assessment</h1> <p><strong>Institution and function:</strong><br> <strong>Disturbance class:</strong><br> <strong>Timescale:</strong><br> <strong>Required distinctions:</strong><br> <strong>Available actions:</strong><br> <strong>Observation channels:</strong><br> <strong>Source dependencies:</strong><br> <strong>Known blind spots:</strong><br> <strong>Rights constraints:</strong><br> <strong>Causal variables and controllers:</strong><br> <strong>Burdens assigned locally:</strong><br> <strong>Upstream and external duties:</strong><br> <strong>Impossible Mandate or Pilot Notice:</strong><br> <strong>Competing assessments:</strong><br> <strong>Possible responses:</strong><br> <strong>Authority able to decide:</strong></p> <h1>Appendix E — Bypass Charter</h1> <p><strong>Problem and affected population:</strong><br> <strong>Authorizing body:</strong><br> <strong>Jurisdiction:</strong><br> <strong>Participants and non-participants:</strong><br> <strong>Rights and protected minimums:</strong><br> <strong>Funding and assets:</strong><br> <strong>Observation Contract:</strong><br> <strong>Causal mechanism and minimum causally adequate scope:</strong><br> <strong>What the pilot cannot establish:</strong><br> <strong>Incumbent interface and response duty:</strong><br> <strong>Duration and review:</strong><br> <strong>Exit:</strong><br> <strong>Possible dispositions:</strong> dissolve / reintegrate / federate / coexist / transfer / succeed<br> <strong>Ordinary owner and maintenance capacity:</strong><br> <strong>Support-withdrawal durability test:</strong><br> <strong>Continuity plan:</strong></p> <h1>Appendix F — Functional Transfer Record</h1> <p><strong>Function:</strong><br> <strong>Current authority:</strong><br> <strong>Receiving authority:</strong><br> <strong>Legal basis:</strong><br> <strong>Reason and alternatives:</strong><br> <strong>Authority formation:</strong><br> <strong>Causal Responsibility Profile:</strong><br> <strong>Response and handoff records:</strong><br> <strong>Affected parties:</strong><br> <strong>Rights and continuity:</strong><br> <strong>Assets and liabilities:</strong><br> <strong>Records and protected knowledge:</strong><br> <strong>Staff obligations:</strong><br> <strong>Review and appeal:</strong><br> <strong>Return or further-change conditions:</strong></p> <h1>Appendix G — Sunset and Dissolution Review</h1> <p><strong>Institution:</strong><br> <strong>Constituting authority:</strong><br> <strong>Mission status:</strong><br> <strong>Continuing need:</strong><br> <strong>Rights effects:</strong><br> <strong>Cost and duplication:</strong><br> <strong>Continuation and closure incentives:</strong><br> <strong>Common-mode dependencies:</strong><br> <strong>Capture indicators:</strong><br> <strong>Alternatives:</strong><br> <strong>Recommendation:</strong><br> <strong>Authorizing process:</strong><br> <strong>Continuity plan:</strong><br> <strong>Dissenting assessment:</strong></p> <h1>Appendix H — Emergency Coordination Record</h1> <p><strong>Emergency and evidence:</strong><br> <strong>Declaring authority:</strong><br> <strong>Coordination charter:</strong><br> <strong>Powers activated:</strong><br> <strong>Powers prohibited:</strong><br> <strong>Affected population and area:</strong><br> <strong>Indigenous and local interfaces:</strong><br> <strong>Protected material minimums:</strong><br> <strong>Data rules:</strong><br> <strong>Start and expiry:</strong><br> <strong>Review:</strong><br> <strong>Restoration:</strong><br> <strong>After-action findings:</strong></p> <h1>Appendix I — Accountable Coordination Audit</h1> <p>For each criterion, provide evidence and counter-evidence.</p> <ol><li><strong>Authority:</strong> revocable and externally grounded, or self-justifying?</li> <li><strong>Transparency:</strong> symmetric, or asymmetric?</li> <li><strong>Exit:</strong> practically protected, or prohibitive?</li> <li><strong>Function:</strong> self-limiting, or self-perpetuating?</li> <li><strong>Decision-chain separation:</strong> are observation, interpretation, authorization, implementation, and adjudication distinct?</li> <li><strong>Protected knowledge:</strong> can the system coordinate without compulsory total legibility?</li> <li><strong>Continuity:</strong> can correction, transfer, or dissolution occur without harming protected rights and services?</li> <li><strong>Causal responsibility:</strong> do responsibility, jurisdiction, capacity, resources, and control over causal variables align?</li> <li><strong>Response ownership:</strong> are acceptance, refusal, delay, capacity limitation, timeout, and constitutional gaps attributable?</li> <li><strong>Common-mode risk:</strong> do nominally separate observers or reviewers share decisive infrastructure, funding, models, or incentives?</li> <li><strong>Documentation proportionality:</strong> is record burden justified by consequence, or is procedural throughput becoming the objective?</li> <li><strong>Outcome and falsification:</strong> what independent evidence would narrow, suspend, replace, or end the coordination mechanism?</li> <li><strong>Representation authority:</strong> are constituency, composition, selection, term, recall, and vacancy rules grounded in the body’s own constituting authority, or is a framework formula masquerading as constitutional authority?</li></ol> <h1>Appendix J — GMEAIA Response, Refusal, and Omission Profile</h1> <p>This appendix is a human-readable Meta-Governance view of GMEAIA-08 and GMEAIA-59. Exact controlled states and lifecycle semantics remain governed by GMEAIA.</p> <p><strong>Request or handoff:</strong><br> <strong>Candidate duty-holder and competence:</strong><br> <strong>Response-duty source and effect:</strong><br> <strong>Response-time assessment:</strong><br> <strong>Acknowledgment deadline:</strong><br> <strong>Decision deadline:</strong><br> <strong>Action or review deadline after acceptance:</strong><br> <strong>Current GMEAIA state:</strong><br> <strong>Reasons, evidence, conflicts, and missing prerequisites:</strong><br> <strong>Expected harm during delay:</strong><br> <strong>Pre-authorized safe-state reference:</strong><br> <strong>Sender duties:</strong><br> <strong>Recipient duties:</strong><br> <strong>Escalation or alternative recipient:</strong><br> <strong>Unresolved-duty custody:</strong><br> <strong>Capacity-limited or constitutional-gap status:</strong></p> <h1>Appendix K — Independence and Objective-Reproduction Review</h1> <p><strong>Institution or mechanism:</strong><br> <strong>Mandate and duration:</strong><br> <strong>Shared funders:</strong><br> <strong>Shared data and observers:</strong><br> <strong>Shared AI, platforms, vendors, and infrastructure:</strong><br> <strong>Shared methods, professional networks, and ontologies:</strong><br> <strong>Independent channel and fallback:</strong><br> <strong>Residual common-mode risk and accepting authority:</strong><br> <strong>Budget, appointment, promotion, prestige, blame, and survival incentives:</strong><br> <strong>Incentives of auditors, facilitators, funders, vendors, and tool stewards:</strong><br> <strong>Behavior likely to regenerate after reform:</strong><br> <strong>Pressures changed by the proposal:</strong><br> <strong>Conditions making the mechanism unnecessary:</strong></p> <h1>Appendix L — Framework Falsification Register</h1> <p><strong>Framework hypothesis:</strong><br> <strong>Expected mechanism:</strong><br> <strong>Independent outcomes:</strong><br> <strong>Disconfirming evidence:</strong><br> <strong>Administrative and latency costs:</strong><br> <strong>Common-mode and selection-pressure risks:</strong><br> <strong>Narrowing condition:</strong><br> <strong>Suspension condition:</strong><br> <strong>Replacement condition:</strong><br> <strong>Non-scaling condition:</strong><br> <strong>Review date and responsible steward:</strong><br> <strong>Dissenting assessment:</strong></p> <hr> <h1>Appendix M — Representation and Composition Migration Note</h1> <p>This appendix is a bounded migration instruction, not a new representation registry or electoral code.</p> <p>Existing GGF frameworks that contain fixed seat ratios, reserved-seat formulas, voting weights, nomination routes, universal demographic quotas, term rules, or claims on another framework’s councils shall review those provisions against §3.7.</p> <p>Each reviewed provision should be classified as one of:</p> <ol><li><strong>validly constituted</strong> — the relevant body’s own constituting instrument lawfully enacts the rule;</li> <li><strong>illustrative design profile</strong> — the framework identifies a representation need or suggested composition without claiming binding authority;</li> <li><strong>external-body prescription requiring removal or adoption</strong> — the framework attempts to compose another independently constituted body and must remove the rule unless that body’s own authority adopts it;</li> <li><strong>Indigenous proxy or quota defect</strong> — the framework assigns Indigenous seats or selectors without authority from the affected peoples and must migrate to §3.8;</li> <li><strong>advisory or expertise role</strong> — the role supplies knowledge or affectedness but should not be described as democratic representation unless a constituency actually delegates authority.</li></ol> <p>Priority migration should begin with provisions that:</p> <ul><li>prescribe composition across Treaty, Meta-Governance, or multiple external councils;</li> <li>claim universal seats across independently constituted bodies;</li> <li>appoint or certify Indigenous representatives through GGF bodies;</li> <li>combine several demographic percentages without explaining constituency, selector, overlap, or amendment;</li> <li>allow a body to alter the rules by which its own members are selected without external constitutional authority.</li></ul> <p>The migration does not require deleting useful domain knowledge about who should be heard. It requires separating <strong>representation requirements</strong> from <strong>constituting authority</strong>.</p> <p>No ordinary electoral-administration machinery is added by this appendix. Ballots, electoral rolls, districting, campaign rules, counting, certification, and recounts remain with the competent electoral authority where elections are actually used.</p> <hr> <h1>Layer 4 — The governance horizon</h1> <h2>1. Governance as a temporary concentration of relationship</h2> <p>Governance is not the opposite of freedom, and freedom is not the absence of every institution. Governance becomes necessary where actions create obligations, where harms cross boundaries, where shared resources require stewardship, and where unequal power makes informal coordination insufficient.</p> <p>But institutions are not reality’s final form. They are temporary concentrations of attention, authority, memory, and response capacity. Their legitimacy depends on what called them into being and on whether they remain answerable to the people, relations, and conditions they affect.</p> <h2>2. Coordination without the fantasy of the whole</h2> <p>No institution can see civilization from nowhere.</p> <p>A global body sees patterns hidden locally and loses distinctions visible on the ground. A local body knows context and may miss downstream effects. Scientific models reveal measurable dynamics and may omit sacred, experiential, or political realities. Traditional Knowledge can preserve long relational memory and must not be extracted into categories that destroy its meaning.</p> <p>Meta-Governance is therefore not the construction of a perfect overview. It is the design of relationships among partial observers who can challenge, complement, and sometimes refuse one another.</p> <h2>3. The intelligence-honor principle</h2> <p>People who perceive systemic contradiction early are often treated as inconvenient, disordered, disloyal, or insufficiently pragmatic. Institutions need ways to hear such perception without declaring every objection correct and without making dissenters carry the full burden of proof.</p> <p>An intelligent system preserves channels through which discomfort can become inquiry rather than retaliation.</p> <h2>4. Natural coordination and its limits</h2> <p>Communities may develop deep capacities for mutual aid, restorative conflict, shared stewardship, and consensual action. Formal governance may recede where relationship and competence make it unnecessary.</p> <p>Yet “natural coordination” must not become a spiritualized excuse for invisible domination, suppressed conflict, gendered care burdens, charismatic authority, or the absence of appeal. Informality is not automatically freedom.</p> <h2>5. The right to refuse</h2> <p>A system proves little by making participation easy for those who already agree with it. Its integrity is tested by what happens to the person, community, institution, or people who say no.</p> <p>Refusal can be mistaken, partial, harmful, principled, protective, or necessary. The task is not to romanticize refusal but to preserve enough material security, standing, and due process that consent remains meaningful.</p> <h2>6. Learning as the capacity to be changed</h2> <p>An institution has not learned merely because it collected more information, issued another report, or adopted new terminology. Learning is visible when evidence changes its model, when challenge changes its procedure, when harm changes its incentives, when delay becomes attributable, and when success allows it to simplify, devolve, or relinquish power.</p> <h2>7. Liberatory impermanence</h2> <p>The highest achievement of a coordination body may be to make itself unnecessary.</p> <p>This does not mean premature dissolution or neglect of public capacity. It means that the body’s identity is not fused with its mission. It may teach a protocol, federate a service, return authority, archive a lesson, and end.</p> <p>The protocol may remain. The institution does not need to become immortal.</p> <h2>8. Governing the governance of governance</h2> <p>Meta-Governance itself must remain inside the field it studies.</p> <p>It has blind spots. Its audits can be captured. Its language can become ritual. Its dashboards can privilege what is easy to observe. Its desire for coherence can erase legitimate difference. Its theory of adaptation can become a justification for permanent intervention.</p> <p>For that reason, the framework’s governing maxims are:</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Meta-Governance may help institutions perceive their interdependence and coordinate their legitimate powers. It may not turn its own perception of the system into a power over the system.</strong></p></blockquote> <blockquote><p><strong>Recognized interdependence must lead to named responsibility, reviewable response, and a lawful path through refusal, incapacity, delay, or constitutional absence—without creating authority by silence.</strong></p></blockquote> <hr> <h1>Conclusion</h1> <p>Integrated Meta-Governance v1.6 retains the ambition of v1.4: governance systems must learn, coordinate, resist capture, experiment, and die gracefully.</p> <p>It changes the constitutional answer to one decisive question: <strong>who gets to act on that diagnosis?</strong></p> <p>The answer is no longer “the Meta-Governance architecture itself.”</p> <p>Observation remains distinct from judgment. Coordination remains distinct from jurisdiction. Experiment remains distinct from succession. Audit remains distinct from dissolution. Foresight remains distinct from command. Representation requirements remain distinct from the authority to constitute the institution that satisfies them.</p> <p>Meta-Governance is strongest not when it becomes the brain of planetary governance, but when it builds reliable interfaces among institutions that remain plural, bounded, contestable, and capable of refusing it.</p> <p>v1.6 adds the complementary discipline that restraint alone is not enough. A constitutionally careful system can still fail when responsibility is assigned without causal control, legitimate authorities do not respond, handoffs time out, observers share one substrate, or coordination institutions reproduce themselves through process rather than outcomes.</p> <p>The standard is therefore not more coordination. It is coordination that names responsibility, respects jurisdiction, operates within the timescale of the condition, reduces rather than exports administrative burden, and can demonstrate when it should become less necessary.</p>",1);function c(e){var i=r();n(1186),o(e,i)}export{c as default};
